# s8144281Assignment2

This is my submission for the NIT3213 final assignment. It's an Android app with
three screens (Login, Dashboard and Details) that gets its data from the
nit3213api (https://nit3213apinew.onrender.com).

# Overview

You log in with my student ID and first name (8144281, Angus). The app then loads a list of
artworks from the API, and you can tap any of them to see the full details.

- Login: sends your details to the API. If something goes wrong, such as a
  wrong password or no internet, a message appears on the screen.
- Dashboard: shows each artwork as a card with its title, artist, medium and
  year.
- Details: shows everything about one artwork, including its description.

Each screen asks the API for fresh data when it opens, so nothing is stored on
the phone.

# Technologies

The app is written in Kotlin and uses Jetpack Compose for the user interface, so
the screens are built with Compose functions instead of XML layouts. Because of
that, the list on the Dashboard is a `LazyColumn` rather than a RecyclerView. It
works the same way, only drawing the rows on screen and reusing them as you
scroll, and each card opens the Details screen when it is tapped. Moving between
screens is handled with Navigation Compose.
I used Jetpack compose because I found tutorials online covering these concepts to
be much clearer than the XML layout ones and easier to impliment.

Other libraries used:

- Hilt for dependency injection
- Retrofit for the API calls
- JUnit for the unit tests

The screens only display things. The ViewModels keep track of what the screen
needs, and the repository is the one place that talks to the API. Hilt provides
the API and repository to the classes that need them, which also lets the tests
use a fake version of the API.

# Running the app

1. Open the project in Android Studio and wait for Gradle to finish syncing.
2. Choose an emulator or a connected device. I used the default Medium Phone.
3. Press Run.
4. Log in with my student ID and first name for credentials (8144281, Angus)

As the API is hosted on a free server, the first request can be slow if the
server has gone to sleep. It may take up to 2-3 minutes, and the app will wait for
it.

# Tests

The unit tests are in `app/src/test`. They cover the repository and the three
ViewModels, including cases like a wrong password or no internet connection.
They use a fake API, so they run without a network connection. To run them,
right-click the test folder and choose Run Tests, or use `./gradlew test`.

# Version control

I used Git while building the app, but my commits were only saved on my own
computer. I've added a screenshot of the commit history to the project in the
dropbox so the progress can be seen.